1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,322
This is ice, near the equator.

2
00:00:02,833 --> 00:00:06,873
In 1988, a satellite saw it
capping the highest ridges

3
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:08,731
of a mountain range in New Guinea.

4
00:00:09,242 --> 00:00:11,773
This is the same place in 2026.

5
00:00:12,098 --> 00:00:14,408
Only a few small fragments are left.

6
00:00:15,012 --> 00:00:18,262
Landsat satellites,
run by NASA and the U.S.

7
00:00:18,286 --> 00:00:22,280
Geological Survey,
have watched Earth since 1972.

8
00:00:22,721 --> 00:00:27,016
The latest one, Landsat
9, lifted off in 2021.

9
00:00:27,341 --> 00:00:30,465
Together, they've watched this ice
for more than forty years.

10
00:00:30,790 --> 00:00:35,608
And scientists now say the last of it
could be gone within a few years.

11
00:00:35,933 --> 00:00:40,577
The ice sits on Puncak Jaya,
in Indonesia's Sudirman Range.

12
00:00:40,751 --> 00:00:43,885
The summit stands
about sixteen thousand feet high,

13
00:00:44,036 --> 00:00:46,521
only about four degrees
south of the equator.

14
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:51,664
Right next to it lies the largest
expanse of warm ocean water on Earth.

15
00:00:52,175 --> 00:00:55,089
To see what this kind
of ice looks like up close,

16
00:00:55,368 --> 00:01:00,639
here's footage from a NASA ice survey,
flying over mountain glaciers in Alaska.

17
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,984
A glacier is a river of ice.

18
00:01:03,158 --> 00:01:06,305
It's thick enough to flow
downhill under its own weight,

19
00:01:06,479 --> 00:01:10,182
and it's fed by snow that piles
up faster than it melts.

20
00:01:10,786 --> 00:01:15,395
Puncak Jaya's glaciers sit near
the warmest conditions in which ice

21
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:17,218
can survive all year.

22
00:01:17,497 --> 00:01:19,389
For thousands of years, it did.

23
00:01:19,714 --> 00:01:22,721
Landsat tracked that ice year by year.

24
00:01:23,046 --> 00:01:27,726
In 1980, there were about six square
kilometers near the highest peaks.

25
00:01:28,004 --> 00:01:33,809
By 1988, about four point three,
roughly one point seven square miles.

26
00:01:34,088 --> 00:01:36,456
That gray scar is the Grasberg mine.

27
00:01:36,630 --> 00:01:42,052
By September 2025, only about zero point
zero nine square kilometers were left.

28
00:01:42,296 --> 00:01:44,072
About a dozen soccer fields.

29
00:01:44,281 --> 00:01:46,591
Roughly two percent of 1988.

30
00:01:46,917 --> 00:01:51,746
At the main monitoring site, the ice
was thirty-two meters thick in 2010,

31
00:01:51,990 --> 00:01:53,697
about a hundred and five feet.

32
00:01:53,906 --> 00:01:56,402
By 2023, four meters.

33
00:01:56,727 --> 00:01:59,768
By November 2024, it was gone.

34
00:02:00,012 --> 00:02:02,566
As glaciers thin, they break apart.

35
00:02:02,717 --> 00:02:06,107
And every new fragment adds
new edges where ice can melt.

36
00:02:06,433 --> 00:02:10,682
Meltwater pools on the surface, like
these ponds on an Alaskan glacier.

37
00:02:11,065 --> 00:02:14,037
On Puncak Jaya, snow still falls.

38
00:02:14,281 --> 00:02:18,263
But on bare rock, it doesn't last
long enough to build new ice.

39
00:02:18,774 --> 00:02:23,035
Chris Shuman, a retired glaciologist
who has tracked these glaciers

40
00:02:23,081 --> 00:02:26,587
in satellite images, calls it
a house of cards problem.

41
00:02:27,028 --> 00:02:31,730
Once this process gets going, he says,
there's really no turning back.

42
00:02:31,974 --> 00:02:37,279
What's left on Puncak Jaya has broken
into small patches that no longer flow.

43
00:02:38,092 --> 00:02:42,330
Donaldi Permana, a climate scientist
at Indonesia's weather agency,

44
00:02:42,504 --> 00:02:45,859
says their life as active
glaciers is effectively over.

45
00:02:46,463 --> 00:02:51,722
Recent satellite studies suggest the main
ice areas could disappear around 2030.

46
00:02:52,163 --> 00:02:55,298
Indonesia's weather agency says
it could happen much sooner,

47
00:02:55,449 --> 00:02:57,725
sped up by the current El Niño.

48
00:02:57,726 --> 00:02:58,585
Here's why.

49
00:02:58,829 --> 00:03:04,320
During El Niño, the air warms
and the freezing level rises above the summit.

50
00:03:04,831 --> 00:03:07,490
So storms bring rain instead of snow.

51
00:03:07,873 --> 00:03:12,343
With less fresh white snow to reflect
sunlight, the ice gets darker,

52
00:03:12,459 --> 00:03:14,944
absorbs more heat and melts faster.

53
00:03:15,327 --> 00:03:18,903
During the 2015 to 2016 El Niño,

54
00:03:19,077 --> 00:03:22,745
the ice thinned about five
times faster than usual.

55
00:03:23,071 --> 00:03:28,539
It lost roughly five point three meters,
about seventeen feet, in a single year.

56
00:03:28,922 --> 00:03:33,404
Scientists call tropical glaciers like
these canaries in the coal mine.

57
00:03:34,007 --> 00:03:38,303
Others are shrinking too:
the Quelccaya Ice Cap in Peru,

58
00:03:38,512 --> 00:03:44,236
the ice fields of Kilimanjaro, and what's
left of Humboldt Glacier in Venezuela.

59
00:03:44,932 --> 00:03:49,065
An aerial survey planned for December
should show how much ice is left

60
00:03:49,123 --> 00:03:50,783
on Puncak Jaya this year.

61
00:03:51,027 --> 00:03:54,905
The El Niño speeding this up
is the same one NASA watched turn

62
00:03:54,975 --> 00:03:56,554
the Pacific red this year.

63
00:03:56,763 --> 00:03:58,191
Watch this one next.
